  "prompt_text": "The figure combines quantum-optics circuit sketches with spacetime cartoons to illustrate how information or correlations can be distributed between two locations, A and B, and compared with a relativistic communication picture. On the left, optical networks made of beam splitters, mirrors, and photon paths are drawn in red, with detectors marked “+1.” Highlighted boxes labeled A and B indicate two spatial modes or parties embedded in interferometric loops. The upper circuit shows a single marked region at a time interacting with a larger path network, while the lower circuit is more symmetric, with A and B appearing twice and crossed paths suggesting two-way or indefinite ordering of routes.\n\nThe middle panels abstract this into spacetime. One shows events at A and B at two times, \\(t_0\\) and \\(t_1\\), linked by curved arrows that suggest repeated exchange or superposed causal connections rather than a single fixed sequence. Another panel depicts a localized wavepacket entering an interferometer-like setup and splitting into alternative paths.\n\nOn the right, cone-shaped spacetime diagrams compare positions of A and B with light-clock references and Earth. The highlighted points lie inside one another’s causal cones at different times, emphasizing how timing and separation determine which event can influence the other.",
